    95	where xp and xc are the reported means for the CHR-P (or CHR-T/APS) and HC (or CHR-NT) groups.
    96	
    97	Finally, we conducted sensitivity analyses on ROIs demonstrating signi /uniFB01 cant effects in the primary analyses, to better elucidate whether identi /uniFB01 ed effects might be better explained in part by factors associated with suboptimal study design as opposed to meaningful neurobiological mechanisms. These analyses included leave-one-out resampling to investigate site effects (eMethods 1), and supplementary testing on an age-, sex-, and site-matched sample (eMethods 2) to control for other potential sources of heterogeneity.
    98	
    99	Person-Based Similarity Index . The personalised estimates of interindividual variability were investigated using the PBSI, calculated according to the formula below, for each SA, CT and SV pro /uniFB01 les [34 -37]. The process for calculating the PBSI scores begins with concatenating the respective regional measures into vectors that represent the pro /uniFB01 le of each speci /uniFB01 c brain phenotype; PBSI-SA, PBSI-CT and PBSI-SV, respectively. This produces a simpli /uniFB01 ed, personalised index for each phenotypic neuroanatomical pro /uniFB01 le. This index can then be used in one of two ways; /uniFB01 rst, to quantify how similar an individuals ' brain pro /uniFB01 le is to that of other individuals with the same clinical pro /uniFB01 le or disorder (within-diagnosis or within-group). Second, to quantify how similar an individuals ' brain pro /uniFB01 le is respective to a normative estimate, i.e. the average of the healthy control group (normativeness) [37].
   100	
   101	<!-- formula-not-decoded -->
   102	
   103	The PBSI of the i th individual is the average correlation between his/her brain measures ( yi ) and the brain measures of any other individual of the reference sample ( yj , for j ≠ i ).
   104	
   105	- (i) Within-group reference: The PBSI-SA, PBSI-CT and PBSI-SV were calculated separately for the CHR-P and HC individuals and thus represent the degree of within-group similarity in these pro /uniFB01 les. Within each group, and for each brain phenotype, Spearman correlation coef /uniFB01 cients were computed between the neuroanatomical pro /uniFB01 le of each participant and the pro /uniFB01 les of each other member of the same group. The average of these coef /uniFB01 cients for each participant yielded their respective PBSI score for each brain phenotype. A higher PBSI score (closer to 1) indicates greater similarity in the neuroanatomical pro /uniFB01 le of an individual to other members of the same group, while a lower score indicates greater deviance in their neuroanatomical pro /uniFB01 le. Group-level comparisons of PBSI-SA, PBSI-CT and PBSI-SV were then conducted between CHRP and HC using one-tailed Welch ' s t tests to examine whether psychosis-risk states were associated with greater within-group variability.
   106	- (ii) Normative reference: Next, the respective neuroanatomical pro /uniFB01 les of each CHR-P individual were correlated with the corresponding pro /uniFB01 les of the members of the HC group, for each brain phenotype. The resulting PBSI scores thus represent the degree of deviation from the ' normative ' range and were transformed into z -scores (PBSI-CT-Z, PBSI-SA-Z, and PBSI-SV-Z). We set &gt;1.5 SD as a threshold to identify individuals at CHR-P who most markedly deviated from the normative neuroanatomical pro /uniFB01 le, in line with previous work [37].
   107	
   108	In both PBSI analyses, we also investigated the potentially moderating effects of transition status (CHR-T/CHR-NT), subgroup status (APS/BLIPS/ GRD), antipsychotic exposure, and overall baseline psychopathology (total CAARMS/SIPS severity z -scores, eMethods 3) on PBSI scores. All multivariable regression models were applied, adjusting for age and sex.
   109	
   110	## RESULTS

